[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: Who may sign a fingerprint should depend on the grade, not be human-only

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-FP-ACCEPT-AUTHORITY
TITLE: Who may sign a fingerprint should depend on the grade, not be human-only
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATUS: open
RETIRES_ON: >>>
A real drift event produces enough edges to make the current rule painful,
which is the first evidence of where the line actually belongs.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
Human-only signing is the current rule and is too blunt. If a couple of nodes
change in a way that touches a hundred edges without meaning anything, and both
ends are llm-authored and llm-graded, an agent should be able to re-sign those
without raising them.

The line is authority, not effort. A contract the operator authored or accepted
escalates to the operator. One that has only ever been llm-graded does not.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The failure mode of human-only is not that it is unsafe, it is that it floods.
A queue of a hundred no-impact acceptances trains the operator to approve
without reading, which is the same rubber stamp the rule exists to prevent,
arrived at from the other direction.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Unsettled: whether the deciding field is the PARENT's authority, the CHILD's,
or either end being human. The parent owns the contract that moved; the child
owns the assumption that may no longer hold. Both readings are defensible and
the answer probably wants a real drift event to look at.

AUTHORED_BY already carries the distinction, so this likely needs no new field.
<<<
